1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://daten.digitale-sammlungen.de/~db/bsb00000558/image_647
2 CONT
2 CONT https://personen.digitale-sammlungen.de//baltlex/Blatt_bsb00000558,00647.html?prozent=
2 CONT
2 CONT II.2
2 CONT
2 CONT =Count Joseph Cornelius O'Rourke=
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Joseph Cornelius O'Rourke (1772-1849) was a Russian nobleman and military leader who fought in the Napoleonic Wars. He is also particularly well remembered in Serbia where he led a combined Russian and Serb army to defeat th
2 CONC e Turks at Varvarin in 1810. A monument in his honor was erected in Serbia in 1910.
2 CONT The O'Rourke family were originally members of the Jacobite Irish Nobility who fled Ireland following defeat by the Williamites in 1691. The majority of the family moved to France, but a branch also moved to Livonia, which was the
2 CONC n a province of the Russian Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT O'Rourke was born at Dorpat in 1772. By this time the family had been completely absorbed into Russian high society, and, according to custom, he was immediately enrolled in the Russian Imperial Guard. He first saw action as a you
2 CONC ng man in Zürich against the French Revolutionary Army and went on to be awarded the Order of Saint George and the Order of St. Anna, serving under General Kutuzov in 1805. Later, O'Rourke raised and equipped his own private regim
2 CONC ent and marched to Serbia to fight the Turks. They fought at Prahovo, Bela Palanka, Soko Bania (Sokobanja) and Jasika before the decisive victory at Varvarin. O'Rourke concluded his military career as a Lieutenant General fighting
2 CONC  French forces in Germany. He retired to his estate at Vselyub near Minsk and died in 1849.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://et.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Cornelius_O'Rourke
